  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+5)^{2}=-20(y+2)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4(-5)(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-5.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-5$ and $k=-2.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-5,-2).$",
